---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { site } from "../config";
import { faqJsonLd } from "../data/faq";
import Faq from "../components/Faq.astro";

const origin = Astro.site ?? new URL(site.url);
const pageUrl = new URL("/blink-rate-detector", origin).toString();
const home = new URL("/", origin).toString();

const breadcrumbs = {
  "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
  itemListElement: [
    {
      "@type": "ListItem",
      position: 1,
      name: "Home",
      item: home,
    },
    {
      "@type": "ListItem",
      position: 2,
      name: "Blink rate detector",
      item: pageUrl,
    },
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title="Blink rate detector for Mac and Windows | EyesRhythm"
  description="EyesRhythm is a webcam blink rate detector for Mac and Windows. It measures how often and how steadily you blink during screen work, on this computer, with no video leaving the machine."
  image="/images/blink-cover.jpg"
  imageAlt="Someone sitting still at a laptop, the kind of long screen stretch a blink rate detector is built for."
  jsonLd={[faqJsonLd(), breadcrumbs]}
>
  <main id="main">
    <header class="cover">
      <img
        src="/images/blink-cover.jpg"
        alt="Someone sitting still at a laptop by a window, looking at the screen for a long stretch of work."
        width="1280"
        height="720"
      />
      <div class="cover__shade"></div>
      <div class="cover__copy">
        <p class="kicker"><span class="kicker__dot"></span>On-device · Mac and Windows</p>
        <h1 class="display">Blink rate detector.</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          EyesRhythm watches your webcam, counts blinks on this computer, and
          turns how often and how evenly you blink into one quiet score.
        </p>
        <div class="btn-row">
          <a class="btn btn--ghost" href={site.downloads.mac.href}>Download for Mac</a>
          <a class="btn btn--ghost" href={site.downloads.windows.href}>Download for Windows</a>
          <a class="btn btn--ghost" href="/journal/camera-setup">Camera setup</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </header>

    <article class="wrap article">
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Focused computer use often cuts blink rate in half. The sting at
          four o’clock is easy not to notice until the eyes already hurt.
          EyesRhythm is a blink rate detector built to sit beside the work
          rather than become another job.
        </p>

        <h2>What a blink rate detector actually measures</h2>
        <p>
          A blink rate detector uses a camera to notice each blink and measure
          how often they happen, usually as blinks per minute. That number
          matters. Office workers in a well-known study blinked about 22 times
          a minute at rest, 10 while reading a book, and 7 while looking at a
          screen. The screen took more than the book.
        </p>
        <p>
          Count alone still misses the hour. Six blinks spread through a minute
          and six blinks piled into clumps, with a long wait in the middle,
          produce the same average and two very different surfaces. EyesRhythm
          folds rate and regularity into the
          <a href="/journal/count-is-not-rhythm">Ocular Rhythm Index</a>, from 0
          to 100.
        </p>

        <h2>How EyesRhythm detects blinks</h2>
        <ol>
          <li>
            Sit as you usually work. A built-in laptop camera or a USB webcam
            is enough, as long as it can see your eyes.
          </li>
          <li>
            The app reads frames on this computer. Nothing is uploaded. Nothing
            is kept as a film of your face.
          </li>
          <li>
            Each counted blink updates the live cadence. Trace shows the last
            minutes. Alert can pulse or make a sound if you ask it to.
          </li>
          <li>
            Practice is a two-minute coach, so a fuller blink is easier to feel
            again after a long stretch of looking.
          </li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          The <a href="/journal/camera-setup">camera setup guide</a> covers
          distance, glasses, and the few things that help the detector count
          cleanly.
        </p>

        <h2>Why screens cut blink rate</h2>
        <p>
          A blink is a short blackout. When the work on the screen is dense,
          the brain parks those blackouts or holds them back. You keep seeing.
          The tear film waits. Incomplete blinks, where the lids never quite
          meet, leave a strip unwiped. That is why a detector that only nags
          you to “blink more” is not the whole picture.
        </p>
        <p>
          The journal piece on
          <a href="/journal/screens-cut-blink-rate">why screens cut your blink rate</a>
          walks through the research, including incomplete blinks and lumpy
          timing. The piece on
          <a href="/journal/jobs-that-dry-the-eyes">jobs that dry the eyes</a>
          is for the kinds of desk work that hold the lids still.
        </p>

        <h2>Private on this computer</h2>
        <p>
          Free for macOS and Windows. No account, no license key, no cloud, and
          no analytics. Camera frames stay on this computer. Today’s totals live
          in a local folder, yours alone.
        </p>
        <p>
          EyesRhythm is a wellness companion, not a medical device. It is not
          intended to diagnose, treat, or prevent disease. If your eyes keep
          hurting or your vision changes, see an eye-care professional.
        </p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <Faq heading="Blink rate detector, in short." />
  </main>
</Layout>

<style>
  .cover {
    position: relative;
    width: 100%;
    min-height: min(72vh, 640px);
    display: grid;
    align-items: end;
    color: #fff;
    background: #1d1d1f;
  }

  .cover img {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    object-fit: cover;
  }

  .cover__shade {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    background: linear-gradient(
      180deg,
      rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.15) 20%,
      rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.62) 100%
    );
  }

  .cover__copy {
    position: relative;
    z-index: 1;
    width: min(var(--wrap), calc(100% - var(--gutter) * 2));
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 48px 0 40px;
    display: grid;
    gap: 12px;
    justify-items: start;
  }

  .cover .kicker {
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.86);
  }

  .cover .display {
    font-size: clamp(2.4rem, 6vw, 4.2rem);
    max-width: 12ch;
    color: #fff;
  }

  .cover .lede {
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.88);
    max-width: 34rem;
  }

  .cover .btn-row {
    margin-top: 4px;
  }

  .cover .btn--ghost {
    color: #fff;
    border-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.35);
  }

  .cover .btn--ghost:hover {
    background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.12);
  }

  .article {
    padding: 48px 0 24px;
    display: grid;
    gap: 16px;
    justify-items: start;
  }
</style>
